<sn><md>### Clone your GitHub repository

First, go to the directory where you want the project:

```bash
cd ~/Documents
```

Then clone it:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/USERNAME/REPOSITORY.git
```

For example:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc.git
```

This creates:

```text
Documents/
└── QDoc/
    ├── .git/
    ├── README.md
    ├── ...
```

Enter the repository:

```bash
cd QDoc
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### 2. Check the connection with GitHub

Run:

```bash
git remote -v
```

You should see something like:

```text
origin  https://github.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc.git (fetch)
origin  https://github.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc.git (push)
```

`origin` is simply the name Git gives to your GitHub repository.

You can also check:

```bash
git status
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### 3. Make changes locally

For example:

```bash
nano README.md
```

or edit the files using your preferred editor.

Then check what changed:

```bash
git status
```

You might see:

```text
modified: README.md
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### 4. Send local changes to GitHub

- Step 1: Stage changes

     ```bash
     git add .
     ```

     `.` means all changed/new files in the current repository.

     You can also add a specific file:

     ```bash
     git add README.md
     ```

-  Step 2: Commit

     ```bash
     git commit -m "Update README"
     ```

     ### Step 3: Push

     ```bash
     git push
     ```

     Now:

     ```text
     Local
       │
       │ git push
       ▼
     GitHub
     ```

</md></sn><sn><md>### 5. Get changes from GitHub

Suppose you changed something on GitHub directly, or another computer pushed changes.

Run:

```bash
git pull
```

This downloads the changes and integrates them into your local branch.

```text
GitHub
  │
  │ git pull
  ▼
Local
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### 6. Your normal daily workflow

If you're working primarily on your Ubuntu PC, a simple workflow is:

- Before starting work

     ```bash
     cd ~/Documents/QDoc
     git pull
     ```
     Work on your files.

- After finishing work

     ```bash
     git status
     git add .
     git commit -m "Describe what I changed"
     git push
     ```

     So the basic cycle is:

     ```text
             ┌──────────────────┐
             │      GitHub      │
             └────────┬─────────┘
                      │
                   git pull
                      │
                      ▼
             ┌──────────────────┐
             │      Local       │
             │   Repository     │
             └────────┬─────────┘
                      │
                   edit files
                      │
                   git add .
                      │
                  git commit
                      │
                   git push
                      │
                      ▼
             ┌──────────────────┐
             │      GitHub      │
             └──────────────────┘
     ```
</md></sn><sn><md>### 7. Important distinction

`git pull` and `git push` do different things:

| Command      | Direction      | Purpose                           |
| ------------ | -------------- | --------------------------------- |
| `git clone`  | GitHub → Local | Get repository for the first time |
| `git pull`   | GitHub → Local | Get newer changes                 |
| `git add`    | Local          | Select changes for commit         |
| `git commit` | Local          | Save changes in Git history       |
| `git push`   | Local → GitHub | Upload commits to GitHub          |
| `git status` | Local          | See current state                 |

The key point is that **GitHub and your local folder don't continuously synchronize automatically**. You explicitly synchronize them with `pull` and `push`.
</md></sn><sn><md>### If you use two computers

For example:

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/Programming/mGit/01.Getting%20Started/assets/github_windows_ubunutu_sync_diagram.jpeg)

On whichever computer you start working:

```bash
git pull
```

After your work:

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "My changes"
git push
```

This is the safest basic workflow for keeping your GitHub repository and local repository synchronized.
</md></sn>